Fix out-of-bounds stack write in tre_parse_bracket_items when a character
class name does not convert to a multibyte string.

A bracket expression whose class name contains characters that cannot be
converted, such as "[[:il\377\377\377\016+:]]+", writes outside a stack
buffer:

    ==1==ERROR: AddressSanitizer: stack-buffer-underflow
    WRITE of size 1
        #0 tre_parse_bracket_items src/extra/tre/tre-parse.c:342
        #1 tre_parse_bracket       src/extra/tre/tre-parse.c:453
        #2 tre_parse               src/extra/tre/tre-parse.c:1388

The class name is converted into the 64-byte stack buffer tmp_str with
wcsrtombs() (or wcstombs()), whose result is assigned to the size_t len
and then used unchecked as "tmp_str[len] = '\0'".  Both functions return
(size_t)-1 when a character does not convert, so len becomes SIZE_MAX and
the store lands far below tmp_str.  A conversion that exactly fills the
buffer is also mishandled: len then equals sizeof(tmp_str) and the store
is one past the end.

Treat a failed conversion as an empty class name -- tre_ctype("") is 0, so
the class is rejected with REG_ECTYPE, which is how other invalid class
names are already handled -- and clamp a full conversion to the last
writable index.

Note this is an out-of-bounds *write*, so it is more serious than the
out-of-bounds read fixed in 0001.  Reachable from R via grep(), sub(),
agrep() and friends whenever the pattern comes from an untrusted source.

Found by fuzzing R with libFuzzer + AddressSanitizer.

Upstream status: NOT YET REPORTED to the R Bugzilla as of 2026-08-05.
Delete this patch once the fix lands in R trunk.

--- a/src/extra/tre/tre-parse.c
+++ b/src/extra/tre/tre-parse.c
@@ -339,6 +339,14 @@
 #else /* !TRE_WCHAR */
 		  strncpy(tmp_str, (const char*)re + 2, len);
 #endif /* !TRE_WCHAR */
+		  /* wcsrtombs()/wcstombs() return (size_t)-1 if the class name
+		     contains characters that do not convert, and may return a
+		     length equal to the buffer size if it converts exactly.
+		     Either way tmp_str[len] would land outside tmp_str. */
+		  if (len == (size_t)-1)
+		    len = 0;
+		  else if (len >= sizeof(tmp_str))
+		    len = sizeof(tmp_str) - 1;
 		  tmp_str[len] = '\0';
 		  DPRINT(("  class name: %s\n", tmp_str));
 		  class = tre_ctype(tmp_str);
